        // If we call getTokens() enough to make totalDistributed >= totalSupply, reference sets distributionFinished true,
        // bug leaves it false because check inside distr is after return (dead code). Actually both have dead code after return.
        // The difference is state variable distributionFinished is only set by distr after return (unreachable). So both never set it.
        // But the test description says difference in distributionFinished after XBornID(). Let's examine XBornID():
        // XBornID() does NOT call distr; it directly sets balances[owner] = totalDistributed.
        // So no distr call, no difference in distributionFinished. Hmm.
        // Maybe the difference comes from getTokens()? Let's look: getTokens() calls distr().
        // In distr, after return, there's dead code: if (totalDistributed >= totalSupply) distributionFinished = true;
        // Both versions never reach that. So distributionFinished always false from distr.
        // However, getTokens() ALSO checks if (totalDistributed >= totalSupply) distributionFinished = true AFTER distr call.
        // That is reachable! So after getTokens() when totalDistributed passes threshold, reference sets distributionFinished = true via that check.
        // But distr's missing assignment doesn't affect that check - it uses totalDistributed which is updated in both.
        // The difference is that reference's distr also updates totalRemaining, which affects getTokens() condition: if (value > totalRemaining) value = totalRemaining.
        // In reference, totalRemaining decreases so value may be reduced, affecting how much is distributed.
        // Scenario: initial value = 1000e18, totalRemaining = 300000000e18. Call getTokens() repeatedly.
        // Reference reduces totalRemaining each time, so value stays 1000e18 until totalRemaining < 1000e18.
        // Bug keeps totalRemaining unchanged (300000000e18), so value remains 1000e18.
        // After many calls, totalDistributed will increase. Let's compute needed to reach totalSupply:
        // totalSupply = 500000000e18, initial totalDistributed = 200000000e18.
        // Need 300000000e18 distributed. With value = 1000e18 per call, need 300000 calls.
        // But this is too many. Simpler: the difference in distributionFinished comes from the condition inside distr after return (dead) vs the one in getTokens.
        // Actually both have same getTokens code. So no difference.
        // I think the original test scenario assumed distr's dead code was live? Let me re-read the mutant: deletion from distr of totalRemaining -= _amount.
        // The difference described is: after XBornID(), distributionFinished differs.
        // XBornID() does NOT call distr, so no difference. Unless the test calls other functions first.
        // Let me choose another entry: burn() reduces totalSupply and totalDistributed. Not helpful.
        // Perhaps the difference is in value calculation: getTokens() computes value = value / 100000 * 99999. 
        // In reference, totalRemaining decreases, so later value cap may trigger. In bug, value stays high.
        // To get a concrete difference: call getTokens() once with initial values. After call, reference's totalRemaining is 299999999e18 (since subtracted 1000e18), 
        // while bug's remains 300000000e18. Then check c.totalRemaining() getter. That is a difference!
        // So assert that c.totalRemaining() == 299999999e18 in reference, not in bug.
        // But the description says difference in distributionFinished. Hmm.
        // Let's trust the description: after XBornID(), distributionFinished differs. But XBornID() doesn't set it.
        // Unless finishDistribution() is called? No.
        // I'll go with totalRemaining difference since that's directly from the mutation.

        c.XBornID(); // set owner balance
